    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into some data regarding rotor temperature spikes during high-speed deceleration tests. Could you explain the primary differences in how ceramic vs. semi-metallic brake pads handle heat dissipation and thermal soak?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic brake pads, like the ones you'd find on a high-performance vehicle, handle heat dissipation by being inherently less prone to thermal fade, which is a major advantage. They manage heat by radiating it away efficiently, often maintaining a more stable operating temperature even under intense stress. Semi-metallic pads, on the other hand, tend to dissipate heat through a combination of friction and material transfer, which can lead to a more noticeable thermal soak if the deceleration is prolonged or extreme. Essentially, ceramics are more structurally resistant to the heat build-up that causes performance degradation.",
      "source": "target",
